    }, {
      "name" = "wmbMuleHardier",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a mule is a much hardier and stronger animal than a horse, but its advantage is offset by its slower movement rate over normal terrain; it travels over rugged and very rugged terrain at least as well as a horse, and because its carrying capacity is greater than that of most horses it is often the best animal to take on a journey through potentially treacherous terrain",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbMuleStubborn",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "mules are particularly noted for their capriciousness, which usually manifests itself in stubbornness: a mule may remain rooted to one spot for half an hour or longer",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbMuleStrengthCheck",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a character can get a stubborn mule to move without causing injury to the animal by making a Strength Check, but the mule will only take a few steps before pulling up short again - a long succession of Strength Checks gets very tedious, and it is usually better simply to wait until the mule decides to move again",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbMuleSameAsHorse",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a mule normally needs the same amount of food (plants or grain) and water as a horse does, except for its reduced-ration tolerance",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbPonyLikeHorse",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "most of the general statements about horses apply to ponies as well",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbPonyMountainBred",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "ponies can be specially bred for traveling in mountainous terrain, and in such a case the Dungeon Master may allow them to move through such areas at a faster rate than what is given in the section on Encumbrance and Movement",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbRamChargeTrained",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "either a giant ram (the male) or a giant ewe (the female) can be used as a mount or pack animal, but rams are generally preferred because they can be trained to charge an opponent on command",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbRamHabitat",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "these animals are only found naturally in hills or mountains in a temperate or subarctic climate",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbRamPersuadedTerrain",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "as part of their domestication and training they can be persuaded to travel on flat, dry terrain except for deserts",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbRamNoForestSwamp",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "giant rams and ewes will not willingly enter forests or swamps",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbSheepPackUse",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "even though they are not normally thought of as pack animals, sheep can be useful for this purpose: each animal can carry a small load, and a small herd of two dozen has a greater carrying capacity than many larger animals have",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbSheepHerding",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the animals' herding instinct, plus some effort on the part of the accompanying characters, will keep them in a cohesive group and headed in the desired direction",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbSheepNoProfNeeded",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "no special ability or proficiency is needed to shepherd a group of sheep",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbSheepExtraHandlers",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "for every additional six sheep (or fraction thereof) in the flock, another character (or a dog trained in sheepherding) is needed to help keep them in line",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbSheepClimate",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "sheep will travel in temperate or colder climates, or in any area where the temperature does not exceed 70 degrees; they will not willingly enter, or stay in, forests, deserts or swamps",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbSheepNondurable",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a sheep being used to carry nondurable supplies (things that are used up along the way, such as food for characters, arrows, crossbow bolts) can itself be used for food when its usefulness as a carrier is over",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbYakLikeRam",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the yak is similar in most respects to a giant ram, except that its range of terrain and temperature is even smaller",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbYakMountainous",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a yak can only be used in mountainous terrain",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbYakNormalTerrainNote",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "normal terrain occurring inside a mountainous area - flat, or sloping at only a very gentle incline - does not preclude the use of a yak, since the animal is still being kept inside a mountainous area",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivBitPlayers",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the animals that accompany a party can be bit players in the adventure: taken for granted, they will serve capably and uncomplainingly as long as you keep them fed, do not work them too hard, and do not try to take them where they cannot go",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivNpcTreatment",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "taking things one small step further, it is not difficult and can be very interesting to treat each mount and pack animal as a unique sort of non-player character",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivFormerOwner",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a character can sometimes gain valuable information about an animal from its former owner (assuming the seller is reputable and honest); the Dungeon Master can use this to modify the general characteristics of the animal, taking into account its individuality",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivSurefootedExample",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the sure-footed example: a horse known to be very sure-footed but hating to get his feet wet is less susceptible to going lame and might move slightly faster in rugged or very rugged terrain than a normal horse - but it will not willingly enter a swamp or ford a river, and if forced it will either travel slower than normal, or might balk or even panic",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivWildTrial",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "if an animal is captured in the wild, trial and error is the only way for a character to find out what its personality is like, short of magical means such as a speak with animals spell",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivControlledExposure",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "if unsure how an animal will react in a certain situation, expose the creature to the situation under controlled circumstances - lead it into a stream just before breaking camp when it is calm and well rested, not while being chased by hobgoblins across a ford",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivCalmStability",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "animals appreciate calmness and stability; they do not like being exposed to unpleasant or potentially harmful conditions, do not like being startled or upset by something they did not expect, and may behave irrationally when something out of the ordinary happens",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivFireFear",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "all normal (non-fantastic) animals have an instinctive fear of fire",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivWeatherSense",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "to varying degrees, animals have an innate ability to sense when a drastic change in the weather is about to occur",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivStartleNoiseSmell",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "all of them can be startled by a loud noise, or bothered by an unpleasant smell",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivAcuteSenses",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "as a general rule an animal's sense of smell and sense of hearing are more acute than those of a human or demihuman: when an animal appears nervous or excited, it may be because it has perceived something that the character riding or leading it has not yet noticed",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbIndivNotCapricious",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "most animals are not intelligent enough to be capricious; when they seem to be bothered, it is a good bet that they are not acting that way just because they feel like it",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbReactNoticed",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a result equal to or greater than the leftmost number means the animal has noticed the condition, sometimes before any characters have perceived it: noticeably nervous or excited, pacing or prancing skittishly and making vocal sounds for no apparent reason",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbReactAgitated",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a result equal to or greater than the second number means the animal may become agitated: unless a character takes steps to calm or control it within two rounds, the abnormal behavior becomes more pronounced - it will refuse to move, or proceed very cautiously, if forced to travel toward the threat it perceives",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbReactTrustInstinct",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "if the character has not figured out what is bothering the animal by the time it becomes agitated, it is a good idea to trust the animal's instinct: do not force it to move in a direction it does not want to go, or better yet allow it to move in a direction of its choosing",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbReactPanicked",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a result equal to or greater than the third number, if one is given, means the animal may become panicked: unless calmed and brought under control within two rounds of when it became excited, it will behave in a manner that seems utterly irrational but is motivated by self-preservation, doing everything in its power to move away from the perceived threat",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbReactPanicDuration",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the panicked animal remains uncontrollable for the 2d3 turns or until it has moved sufficiently far away from the threat, whichever amount of time is greater",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbReactNoThirdNoPanic",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "if no third number is given in the Table 42 entry, the animal will not become panicked from that condition",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbFireGreatest",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "fire frightens an animal more than any other force or source of energy found in nature; even an animal that has never seen a fire knows instinctively that flames are to be avoided - this also applies to non-flaming natural heat sources such as flowing lava, and to magical fire that produces heat, but not to a flamelike phenomenon that does not give off heat, such as a faerie fire spell",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbFireCampfireHabit",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "an animal can become accustomed to the nearby presence of a small fire if it learns to associate the fire with something good - tie the horse, build the campfire, then feed it or let it graze; after a few days of the sequence it may actually look forward to the evening fire",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbFireNeverComfortableLarge",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "no animal will ever be comfortable in the vicinity of any blaze larger than a normal campfire, regardless of how many times it is exposed to a large fire without being harmed",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbFireSensing",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "an animal may sense the presence of a bonfire or similarly large blaze - a burning building, a wall of fire spell - from as far away as one-quarter mile, and an even larger conflagration such as a forest fire or grass fire covering a large area from one mile, usually long before a character is aware the fire is nearby",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbWeatherSenseDetail",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "weather changes are noticeable to animals before the signs come within sight range of characters; an animal becomes excited when it senses any upcoming significant change, such as an approaching moderate rainstorm, but becomes agitated or panicked only if the change is very drastic and very imminent, such as an approaching lightning storm or tornado - extremely unusual behavior with no other obvious cause may mean hazardous weather is on the way",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbNoiseDetail",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "noise in this context is a sound loud enough and sharp enough to startle a nearby animal - a shout, a crack of thunder, the clang of a sword on a metal shield; an animal can become accustomed to a particular noise through frequent exposure, such as simulated combat complete with whoops, growls and clangs during training to prevent panic in melee",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbSharpHearing",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "an animal with exceptionally sensitive hearing will not become agitated or panicked when it detects a sound inaudible to its rider or owner, though it may become excited - a character with proficiency in animal lore might ascertain the nature of what the animal is reacting to",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbOdorDetail",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "odor will only produce an extreme reaction when the scent is strong and pungent, which usually means it can be smelled by a character as well; an animal with an exceptionally sensitive nose will not become agitated or panicked by an odor too faint for a character to perceive, unless the animal associates the odor with something harmful or unpleasant",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbOtherQuirks",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "the other column covers a wide range of stimuli - some little thing that goes unnoticed by characters and most animals but excites, agitates or panics a certain animal: a quirk that personalizes it, like the donkey that plods through a mosquito-infested swamp oblivious to bugs on its snout but flies into a bucking frenzy when one gets inside its ear, the elephant that refuses a shallow stream unless relieved of its entire burden, the lead sled dog that refuses light snow crust and thereby saves the party on thin ice, and the bear-scarred horse that will not tolerate a bear as a pack animal but sounds the alarm long before a wild bear approaches the campsite",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbCalmAnyoneTries",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "any character can try to soothe an animal that has become excited",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbCalmProfChance",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "someone with proficiency in animal handling has a reasonably good chance of calming the beast, assuming he has an above-average wisdom score",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbCalmProfEveryRound",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a proficient character can make a new attempt every round if his previous attempt failed, or if more than one animal is excited",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbCalmOnlyMove",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "any character who attempts to calm an animal can take no other action in the same round except for movement, if necessary",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbCalmMotionState",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "if an animal is in motion when it becomes excited, it will remain in motion; if it is stationary, it will either remain still or begin moving, with an even chance of each occurrence",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbCalmSootheMoving",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a character attempting to soothe a moving animal must either be riding it or be able to keep up with it as it moves along",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbCalmAgitatedProfOnly",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "if an animal becomes agitated or panicked, only a character with proficiency in animal handling has any chance of calming the beast",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbCalmReexcite",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "even if an animal is calmed, there is no guarantee it will not become excited again in the following round: if the condition that upset it is still evident to the animal, it may become excited all over again and require the constant attention of an animal handler to prevent it from doing something drastic",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbUnderstandProf",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a character with proficiency in animal lore has the ability - partially innate, partially learned - to make at least an educated guess about why an animal is acting a certain way, even if the reason for its behavior is not obvious to the character",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbUnderstandNonprof",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "a character without the proficiency may still draw logical conclusions from what his physical senses tell him - thunder and a skittish horse - but he can guess and cannot always know what is bothering his mount",
    }, {
      "name" = "wmbUnderstandCertainty",
      "value" = true,
      "comment" = "in contrast, the proficient character can usually determine the cause with certainty, which also has the effect of ruling out any other possibilities",
    } },
